Q: Buffer Overflow Shell A: <p>My recommendation would be to avoid copying and rather do a compare. Using <code>strrchr</code> finds the last dot, which is correct for multi-dot filenames like <code>index.final.html</code>.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;string.h&gt;

int verify_extension(const char *fname) {
    const char *dot = strrchr(fname, '.');
    if (!dot || dot[1] == '\0') {
        return -1; 
    }
    // Compare exactly to &quot;html&quot; and ensure the string ends there
    return (strcmp(dot + 1, &quot;html&quot;) == 0) ? 0 : 1;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Or copy into a bounded buffer. <code>strrchr</code> ensures you are parsing the correct extension. <code>strnlen</code>/bounded copy prevents writing past <code>extension</code>.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;string.h&gt;

int verify_extension(const char *fname) {
    const char *dot = strrchr(fname, '.');
    if (!dot || dot[1] == '\0') return -1;

    char extension[16];  
    size_t n = strnlen(dot + 1, sizeof(extension));
    if (n &gt;= sizeof(extension)) return -1;

    memcpy(extension, dot + 1, n);
    extension[n] = '\0';
    return strcmp(&quot;html&quot;, extension); 
}
</code></pre>
<p>Hope it gives you clarity.</p>

Q: awk - &quot;transpose-like&quot; data transformation A: <p>Using any <code>awk</code> plus <code>column</code> to make the output tabular:</p>
<pre><code>$ cat tst1.awk
BEGIN { FS=OFS=&quot;;&quot; }
NR == 1 { next }
!seen[$1]++ { dates[++numDates] = $1 }
!seen[$2]++ { fqdns[++numFqdns] = $2 }
{ labels[$1, $2] = $3 }
END {
    printf &quot;%s&quot;, &quot;DATE&quot;
    for (fqdnNr = 1; fqdnNr &lt;= numFqdns; fqdnNr++) {
        printf &quot;%s%s&quot;, OFS, fqdns[fqdnNr]
    }
    print &quot;&quot;
    for (dateNr = 1; dateNr &lt;= numDates; dateNr++) {
        date = dates[dateNr]
        printf &quot;%s&quot;, date
        for (fqdnNr = 1; fqdnNr &lt;= numFqdns; fqdnNr++) {
            fqdn = fqdns[fqdnNr]
            key = date SUBSEP fqdn
            if ( key in labels ) {
                skipCnt[fqdn] = 0
                label = labels[date,fqdn]
            }
            else if ( skipCnt[fqdn] == &quot;&quot; ) { label = &quot;nil&quot;  }
            else if ( ++skipCnt[fqdn] &lt; 3 ) { label = &quot;null&quot; }
            else                            { label = &quot;warn&quot; }
            printf &quot;%s%s&quot;, OFS, label
        }
        print &quot;&quot;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<pre><code>$ awk -f tst1.awk file | column -s';' -t
DATE        www.site1.tld  www.site2.tld  www.site3.tld
2025-11-01  low            high           nil
2025-11-02  null           mid            nil
2025-11-04  mid            low            nil
2025-11-05  mid            high           init
2025-11-06  mid            null           null
2025-11-08  high           low            null
2025-11-09  low            null           warn
</code></pre>
<p>The above assumes that by &quot;days&quot; in</p>
<blockquote>
<p>when 3 consecutive days with null are found...</p>
</blockquote>
<p>you mean rows in your input. If instead you mean calendar dates then using GNU awk for <code>gensub()</code> and <code>mktime()</code> functions:</p>
<pre><code>$ cat tst2.awk
BEGIN { FS=OFS=&quot;;&quot; }
NR == 1 { next }
!seen[$1]++ { dates[++numDates] = $1 }
!seen[$2]++ { fqdns[++numFqdns] = $2 }
{ labels[$1, $2] = $3 }
END {
    printf &quot;%s&quot;, &quot;DATE&quot;
    for (fqdnNr = 1; fqdnNr &lt;= numFqdns; fqdnNr++) {
        printf &quot;%s%s&quot;, OFS, fqdns[fqdnNr]
    }
    print &quot;&quot;
    for (dateNr = 1; dateNr &lt;= numDates; dateNr++) {
        date = dates[dateNr]
        printf &quot;%s&quot;, date
        for (fqdnNr = 1; fqdnNr &lt;= numFqdns; fqdnNr++) {
            fqdn = fqdns[fqdnNr]
            key = date SUBSEP fqdn
            if ( key in labels ) {
                label = labels[date,fqdn]
                prevDate[fqdn] = date
            }
            else if ( prevDate[fqdn] == &quot;&quot; ) {
                label = &quot;nil&quot;
            }
            else {
                prevSecs = mktime(gensub(/-/,&quot; &quot;,&quot;g&quot;,prevDate[fqdn])&quot; 12 0 0&quot;)
                currSecs = mktime(gensub(/-/,&quot; &quot;,&quot;g&quot;,date)&quot; 12 0 0&quot;)
                diffSecs = currSecs - prevSecs
                diffDays = int( diffSecs / (24 * 60 * 60) + 0.5 )
                label = ( diffDays &lt;= 3 ? &quot;null&quot; : &quot;warn&quot; )
            }
            printf &quot;%s%s&quot;, OFS, label
        }
        print &quot;&quot;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<pre><code>$ awk -f tst2.awk file | column -s';' -t
DATE        www.site1.tld  www.site2.tld  www.site3.tld
2025-11-01  low            high           nil
2025-11-02  null           mid            nil
2025-11-04  mid            low            nil
2025-11-05  mid            high           init
2025-11-06  mid            null           null
2025-11-08  high           low            null
2025-11-09  low            null           warn
</code></pre>
<p>The output is the same for both scripts because the provided sample input isn't adequate to test the difference between the 2 possible meanings of &quot;days&quot;. If we modify the input to create 3 days between the final 2 rows:</p>
<pre><code>$ cat file2
DATE;FQDN;LABEL
2025-11-01;www.site1.tld;low
2025-11-01;www.site2.tld;high
2025-11-02;www.site2.tld;mid
2025-11-04;www.site1.tld;mid
2025-11-04;www.site2.tld;low
2025-11-05;www.site1.tld;mid
2025-11-05;www.site2.tld;high
2025-11-05;www.site3.tld;init
2025-11-06;www.site1.tld;mid
2025-11-08;www.site1.tld;high
2025-11-08;www.site2.tld;low
2025-11-12;www.site1.tld;low
</code></pre>
<p>then we can see a difference in the output of both scripts (note <code>null</code> vs <code>warn</code> at the bottom of the middle column):</p>
<pre><code>$ awk -f tst1.awk file2 | column -s';' -t
DATE        www.site1.tld  www.site2.tld  www.site3.tld
2025-11-01  low            high           nil
2025-11-02  null           mid            nil
2025-11-04  mid            low            nil
2025-11-05  mid            high           init
2025-11-06  mid            null           null
2025-11-08  high           low            null
2025-11-12  low            null           warn
</code></pre>
<pre><code>$ awk -f tst2.awk file2 | column -s';' -t
DATE        www.site1.tld  www.site2.tld  www.site3.tld
2025-11-01  low            high           nil
2025-11-02  null           mid            nil
2025-11-04  mid            low            nil
2025-11-05  mid            high           init
2025-11-06  mid            null           null
2025-11-08  high           low            null
2025-11-12  low            warn           warn
</code></pre>

Q: JSF commandbutton not being called when adding primefaces ajax A: <p>I would suggest to remove the <code>&lt;p:ajax&gt;</code> and just use the <code>&lt;p:commandButton&gt;</code> oncomplete attribute directly:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;p:commandButton value="Register" actionListener="#{ClientMB.Register()}" oncomplete="forGo()" /&gt;
</code></pre>
